 In this article, we introduce a new algorithm for conjunctive query evaluation which achieves, for each Boolean conjunctive query
 Q
 and for all ε > 0, a running time of
 O
 (
 N
 
 subw(
 Q
 )+ ε)
 
 . This new algorithm's description and analysis are, in our view, significantly simpler than those of PANDA. We refer to it as a
 primal
 algorithm as it operates in the primal space of the described maximization problem, by throughout maintaining a feasible primal solution, namely, a polymatroid. Indeed, this algorithm deals directly with the input conjunctive query and adaptively computes a sequence of joins, in a guided fashion, so that the cost of these join computations is bounded. Additionally, this algorithm can achieve the stated runtime for the generalization of the submodular width incorporating degree constraints. We dub our algorithm
 Jaguar
 , as it is a join-adaptive guided algorithm.
